Yes. Waiting is still a move, and time is still charging you for it.

The mistake is treating inaction as neutral. It usually isn’t. While you wait, conditions change, opportunities decay, other players move, your information gets stale, and sometimes the cost of acting rises.

But that doesn’t make all waiting bad. There’s a difference between idle waiting and positioning. A fisherman can sit still while actively reading the tide, preserving energy, changing bait, or letting the right window develop. From the outside, nothing happens; strategically, plenty does.

So the sharper rule is:

The clock runs during both action and inaction. Make sure waiting is buying you something.

Or even shorter:

If you’re going to wait, make the wait work.